.env
node_modules/
web/dist/
*.log
.DS_Store
# Runtime output: uploaded source files plus the search index. **This line has no leading
# slash.** A leading slash on `/data` would match only the repository root, but the server
# creates a `data/` directory wherever it starts. Without the leading slash, this line also
# ignores `web/data`, which leaked into a past commit with 30 files, including two uploaded test documents.
data/
# docs/ is a local drafting area. Only the decision records and the pipeline reference are tracked.
# This line must read `/docs/*`, not `/docs`. When git ignores a parent directory, a `!` rule
# cannot bring a path back inside it.
/docs/*
!/docs/decisions/
!/docs/pipeline.md
.claude/

# Runtime output from the benchmark harness and temporary scripts. **These lines ignore a
# whole prefix.** An earlier version of this file listed only `_bench_*`, and `_holmes2.json`
# was committed by mistake as a result.
_*.json
_*.err
_*.sse
_*.tmp
_*.out
_*.sh
_*.txt

# wiki-history is test corpus data. Neither the text nor the manifest belongs in this
# repository: the text is about 7 MB of CC BY-SA text, and the manifest is the output of one
# sampling run. To reproduce it, run fetch-wiki-history.mjs. The `--manifest` flag writes a
# manifest, and `--from-manifest` rebuilds the corpus byte-for-byte from that manifest
# (`action=parse&oldid` never changes).
/scripts/bench/corpora/wiki-history.json
/scripts/bench/corpora/wiki-history.manifest.json
# The same rule applies to a subset built by subset-corpus.mjs: it is the output of a manifest
# plus one command, not source material. The command belongs in the test record; the corpus itself does not.
/scripts/bench/corpora/wiki-*-subset.json
/scripts/bench/corpora/wiki-nov2023.json
